\description{
Estimates small area parameters using unit-level Hierarchical Bayesian models
(Battese-Harter-Fuller model and generalized linear mixed models)
with Integrated Nested Laplace Approximations (\pkg{INLA}) or by full MCMC
(\code{\link{jax_engine}()}, NumPyro NUTS). Supports Gaussian,
Binomial (logistic regression for binary unit responses), and Poisson likelihoods,
with optional spatial random effect structures (BYM2, Besag) and finite
population adjustments.
}
\details{
\strong{Non-Gaussian families.} The non-sampled part of a domain mean is predicted at the
mean covariates of the non-sampled units, \eqn{g^{-1}(\bar x_{d,r}^\top\beta + u_d)}. For the
identity link of \code{"gaussian"} this is the mean of \eqn{x_j^\top\beta + u_d} over the
units. For the logit and log links of \code{"binomial"} and \code{"poisson"} it is not: the
mean of \eqn{g^{-1}(x_j^\top\beta + u_d)} over the units differs from it whenever the
covariates vary within the domain (for the log link it is larger by the factor
\eqn{E[\exp((x_j - \bar x)^\top\beta)] > 1}). In a simulation with one normal covariate of
within-domain variance 1, the domain means of the sampled domains were biased by -8\%
(binomial, slope 1.2) and -16\% (Poisson, slope 0.6), and the 95\% credible intervals
covered the true means in 90\% and 78\% of the cases. Unbiased estimation needs the covariates
of every population unit, as in the empirical best predictor of Molina and Rao (2010); a
unit-level \code{Xpop} is currently averaged per domain. A warning is given for these families.
}
\section{Engines}{

Both engines fit the same model from the sampled units, with the same default priors, and
return the same structure. The standard deviations of the domain effect and, for
\code{"gaussian"}, of the unit errors have half-normal priors with scale \eqn{s_y}, the
standard deviation of the sampled unit responses (scale 1 on the link scale for
\code{"binomial"} and \code{"poisson"}), and the coefficients \eqn{N(0, 1000)} priors.
\code{jax_engine(priors = "weakly_informative")} uses half-normal(1) standard deviations and
\eqn{N(0, 10^2)} coefficients (\eqn{N(0, 2.5^2)} for \code{"binomial"}) on the standardised
scale instead. See \code{vignette("hb-priors", package = "fastsae")}. The domain means, SDs and 2.5\%/97.5\% quantiles are computed from the MCMC
draws; with \code{popsize_var} each draw of the Gaussian domain mean includes the mean error of
the non-sampled units, \eqn{N(0, \sigma_e^2 / (N_d - n_d))} for that draw of \eqn{\sigma_e^2}
(the INLA engine adds its posterior mean to the variance and gives a normal interval).

The following comparisons were made with the earlier default priors (half-normal(1) on the
standardised scale for \code{jax_engine()}, INLA's log-gamma priors on the precisions). On simulated data (30 domains, three unsampled) a Stan fit of the same model and priors gave
domain means within 0.04 posterior SD and SDs within 5\% for the Gaussian (also with BYM2),
Bernoulli and Poisson models. With their own priors, \code{hbsae::fSAE(method = "HB")}
(Gaussian, numerical integration over the variance ratio, flat priors) and the INLA engine
gave domain means within 0.1 and 0.06 posterior SD. With few domains the prior of the domain
variance matters: on \code{cornsoybean} (12 counties) the county SDs of the JAX engine were
1.1-1.4 times those of INLA under these earlier priors. With the current shared priors they
are 0.98-1.06 times those of INLA, and both engines match the exact posterior (INLA within
0.06 and JAX within 0.14 posterior SD).
}

\examples{
\donttest{
if (requireNamespace("INLA", quietly = TRUE)) {
  library(fastsae)
  data(cornsoybean)
  data(cornsoybeanmeans)

  # Prepare auxiliary population means
  df_pop <- cornsoybeanmeans
  names(df_pop)[names(df_pop) == "MeanCornPixPerSeg"] <- "CornPix"
  names(df_pop)[names(df_pop) == "MeanSoyBeansPixPerSeg"] <- "SoyBeansPix"

  # Unit survey data
  df_sample <- cornsoybean
  names(df_sample)[names(df_sample) == "County"] <- "CountyIndex"

  # Fit unit-level Hierarchical Bayes model
  fit_hb_u <- hb_unit(
    formula = CornHec ~ CornPix + SoyBeansPix,
    unit_data = df_sample,
    Xpop = df_pop,
    domain_var = "CountyIndex",
    popsize_var = "PopnSegments"
  )
  print(fit_hb_u)
}
}
}
